; lang.xon -- what this bundle IS.
;
; xon: read with the ordinary reader and never evaluated, like every other
; manifest in the x-lang world (pin.xon, engine.pin.xon).  A form a reader
; does not know is a loud error, never a skip -- a manifest that silently
; ignores half of itself cannot be extended without wondering which readers
; obeyed which half.
;
; FORMAT (one form per line):
;   (lang "NAME")               the -l name
;   (dialect NAME)              he | xe | rn -- a REQUIREMENT, not a taste
;   (requires-release "TAG")    the x-lang this bundle was built against
;   (requires-lang "NAME" ["V"]) another lang this one is written on top
;                               of, optionally at an exact version
;   (entry "FILE")              loaded AFTER the dialect, not instead of it

(lang "r5rs")

; XENON, derived from a measurement rather than a preference.  Scheme's numerics
; ARE the tower: string->number falls back to %float, and 20-numeric-tower
; exercises rationals and complexes.
;
; PORTS DID NOT MOVE IT, though the note in scm/ports.scm predicted they would.
; That prediction was about the 2024 implementation, which used dlopen -- a
; radon opt-in.  The rewrite drives File instead, and r5rs/base.x reaches it
; with an explicit (import x/sys/file): a dialect decides what is PRELOADED,
; not what is reachable, so importing a module the dialect skips is ordinary
; rather than a violation.
;
; Measured both ways rather than reasoned about: 667/16 under xe and 667/16
; under rn, with ports loaded in each.  The dialect row is a requirement, so it
; should only move when something actually requires it to.
(dialect xe)

; PAIRING, NOT PINNING.  A digest freezes bytes; it says nothing about whether
; those bytes still run against a platform that moved.  This row is the part
; that is checkable, and it is compared for EQUALITY, never parsed.
;
; v0.6.0 was the first release carrying what this bundle needs: bundle
; acquisition, `-l` loading, --share-dir answering from any cwd, and eval! in
; the seam.  This row tracks what these specs are green AGAINST rather than the
; floor -- the question it answers is "what was this built against", not "what
; is the oldest that would still work".
;
; v0.9.0 CARRIES THE DECIMAL TOWER, and pairing with it took a change in the
; SUITE rather than in this bundle.  x/num/decimal adds ~50M objects a load, and
; the runner buckets up to SPEC_BATCH files into one process with no collection
; between them -- so a bucket crossed the runaway ceiling and batches died
; (667/34) without a line here being wrong.  CI runs SPEC_BATCH=1, one file per
; process, and the suite is 667/0 against v0.9.0's ceiling.  Measured, not
; assumed: forced to the same 300M limit at SPEC_BATCH=1, this suite is green.

; v0.14.0 IS THE FIRST RELEASE THIS SUITE CAN BOOT FROM AN IMAGE, and that is
; the whole of why this row moves.  tests/spec-runner.sh images the harness when
; the platform can write one and boots from source when it cannot; two platform
; defects made "cannot" the answer on every release before this one, and each
; failed in a way that named nothing near itself.
;
;   x-lang#643.  (%image-recache!) walked its hook list with a `do` whose first
;   operand is a LIST OF PAIRS -- which this bundle's own `do` has to read as
;   R5RS iteration, since scm/derived.scm dispatches on shape.  So the walk
;   visited every hook and CALLED NONE, in silence: every value the writer had
;   nil'd as a transient stayed nil, and an image-booted suite answered 107 of
;   667 wrong, the first 34 saying `ffi-call s0->d: nil` -- float.x's strtod,
;   never remade.  From source the hooks are never called at all, which is why
;   the suite was green on both sides of it.
;
;   x-lang#651.  float.x closed over the dlsym result for each libm entry, so a
;   raw address sat inside each closure's frame -- where the transient rule
;   cannot reach it, because %image-transients names GLOBALS.  The writer
;   refused with `unnameable: 16`.  ON LINUX ONLY: glibc keeps a dlopen'd libm
;   out of the global scope, where macOS's libSystem folds it in, so the same
;   words that have no name under glibc resolve and are named there.  A clean
;   `unnameable: 0` on a mac was that bug passing quietly, not its absence.
;
; MEASURED against v0.14.0, whole suite, this bundle at the commit that moves
; this row, each a full run:
;
;   from source (IMG=0 SPEC_BATCH=1)   667/0   4m40s
;   from the image                     667/0     45s
;
; The probes in tests/spec-runner.sh stay, and are not redundant with this row:
; they test for the DEFECTS rather than for a version, so a platform that has
; neither fix still boots from source and says which one it lacks.
(requires-release "v0.14.0")

(entry "run.x")
